Draft:
On August 10, 2022, the Iranian government-sponsored MuddyWater Advanced Persistent Threat (APT) group was observed attempting to gain unauthorized access to sensitive government and commercial networks. The MuddyWater APT group utilized the PowGoop malware to facilitate the intrusion. Malicious actors employed tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs) to carry out the attack, including the use of remote services (T1204.002), spear phishing via email (T1566.001), and the execution of malicious code on a compromised host (T1574.002). Additionally, the attackers exploited existing vulnerabilities to maintain persistence and command and control capabilities (T1059.001) and conducted network scanning to identify potential targets (T1027). The MuddyWater APT group also used DNS tunneling (T1036.005) to evade detection. To mitigate this threat, organizations are advised to apply the mitigations outlined in this advisory and review the following resources for additional information. These resources provide detailed guidance on hardening network defenses and identifying indicators of compromise associated with the MuddyWater APT group's tactics, techniques, and procedures.

