Draft:
From April 2022, the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) and other agencies have observed threat activity from two threat actors, Threat Actor 1 (TA1) and Threat Actor 2 (TA2). This joint Cybersecurity Advisory (CSA) provides the tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs) and indicators of compromise (IOCs) associated with the activity. The advisory is based on an incident response engagement conducted by CISA and a third-party incident response organization. The joint effort identified that the threat actors targeted public-facing servers, including one running VMWare Workspace ONE Access, and exploited the vulnerability identified in CVE-2022-22960. The actors used a malicious shell script to gain initial access and, subsequently, deployed a Dingo J-spy webshell to maintain persistence. Additionally, the actors used process injection [T1071.001] and exploited the PrintNightmare vulnerability [T1068] to escalate privileges and gain domain administrator access. The actors also employed tactics to evade detection, including masquerading their malware and tools as legitimate files [T1036] and using process injection [T1055]. The actors' primary objective was to exfiltrate sensitive data and delete evidence of compromise. To mitigate these threats, CISA recommends that organizations patch for CVE-2022-22960 and implement security best practices, including secure configuration and network segmentation. This advisory is based on the MITRE ATT&CK for Enterprise framework, version 11.

