Draft:
The continued emphasis on administrative privileges and access control as critical vectors in cyber operations highlights the ongoing threat landscape faced by organizations. Adversaries, regardless of their affiliation, have demonstrated the ability to exploit weak access controls and administrative privileges to gain initial access and maintain persistence within networks. CISA has observed that a significant number of compromise incidents can be attributed to improper management of administrative credentials, including scenarios where default or weak credentials were utilized, or where administrative access was overly liberal and not properly segmented. To mitigate these risks, CISA recommends a series of best practices. Organizations should segregate and control administrative privileges, ensuring that administrative credentials are restricted to specific functions or time-limited sessions. It is also crucial to remove Domain Administrator (DA) and Enterprise Administrator (EA) accounts from the local-administrators group on all devices. Additionally, UNIX-based systems should have sudo (or root) access tightly restricted to only the most necessary accounts. Employing multifactor authentication for admin accounts further enhances security posture. Disabling unencrypted remote-administrative protocols and services and authorizing and implementing the most secure version of required protocols are also essential. CISA analysts have noted that implementing these measures can significantly reduce the risk of unauthorized access and maintain the integrity and security of organizational networks.

