Draft:
Effective Mitigations Against Advanced Persistent Threats (APT) • Application whitelisting (AWL) is a critical measure to prevent unauthorized software from executing. By defining a list of approved applications, AWL can prevent malicious or unauthorized software from running, thereby reducing the risk of APTs exploiting vulnerabilities. Organizations should implement AWL across all endpoints, ensuring that only trusted applications are permitted to execute. • Defense-in-depth involves layering multiple security controls to enhance overall security posture. This approach can include firewalls, intrusion detection and prevention systems, and other security tools that work in conjunction to detect and mitigate threats. By layering security controls, organizations can better protect against APTs that may evade single-point security measures. • Directory whitelisting restricts access to specific directories, ensuring that only authorized files and processes can be accessed. This can help prevent APTs from executing malicious code or accessing sensitive data. Implementing directory whitelisting can significantly reduce the risk of APTs compromising systems. • The Microsoft Software Restriction Policy (SRP) is a powerful tool for enhancing security by controlling application execution. SRP allows administrators to define security levels and restrict which applications can run based on their digital signatures or other attributes. By implementing SRP, organizations can further reduce the risk of APTs executing malicious code. • AppLocker is another effective tool for controlling application execution. Unlike SRP, AppLocker allows administrators to specify which files and applications users can run based on the file path or digital signature. This can help prevent APTs from executing malicious code by ensuring that only trusted applications can run. • Safe defaults involve configuring systems to their most secure settings out-of-the-box. This includes setting permissions, disabling unnecessary services, and configuring security policies to their most restrictive levels. By implementing safe defaults, organizations can reduce the risk of APTs exploiting misconfigurations or default settings. For more detailed information on implementing these mitigations, refer to the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) Special Publication 800-53 Revision 5 and the Center for Internet Security (CIS) Critical Security Controls. These resources provide comprehensive guidance on configuring and enforcing these security measures to enhance the overall security posture against APTs.

